[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Process Death, State Restoration, and SavedStateHandle in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Android development, process death occurs when the operating system terminates your app's background process to reclaim memory for foreground tasks. While configuration changes like screen rotations destroy and recreate UI controllers, process death wipes out the entire app process, destroying all in-memory variables. This tutorial explores how to handle process death gracefully using SavedStateHandle."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Lifecycle of Process Death"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When your app is backgrounded, the OS places it in a cached state. If the system runs low on memory, it kills the process without executing any lifecycle callbacks like onStop() or onDestroy(). When the user navigates back to your app, the system creates a new process and restores the top Activity on the back stack. However, ViewModels are recreated from scratch, meaning standard non-persisted states are lost."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of SavedStateHandle"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"SavedStateHandle is a key-value map that allows you to write and retrieve data to and from the saved state mechanism. This data survives process death automatically. It is integrated directly into Jetpack ViewModel, making it the ideal tool for preserving critical UI state across process terminations."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing SavedStateHandle in a ViewModel"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use SavedStateHandle, simply inject it into your ViewModel's constructor. Hilt and ViewModelProvider handle this dependency injection automatically."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class UserViewModel(private val savedStateHandle: SavedStateHandle) : ViewModel() {

    // Retrieve state or provide a default value
    val searchQuery: StateFlow<String> = savedStateHandle.getStateFlow(SEARCH_QUERY_KEY, "")

    fun updateSearchQuery(query: String) {
        savedStateHandle[SEARCH_QUERY_KEY] = query
    }

    companion object {
        private const val SEARCH_QUERY_KEY = "search_query"
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for State Restoration"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Only store lightweight data in SavedStateHandle, such as IDs, navigation arguments, and simple user inputs."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid storing large objects, database connections, or Bitmaps in SavedStateHandle to prevent transaction too large binder exceptions."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine SavedStateHandle with Kotlin StateFlow to maintain a reactive and persistent UI data layer."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Testing Process Death"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can easily simulate process death during development using ADB. Open your terminal and run the following command while your app is in the background:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"adb shell am kill com.your.package.name"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you reopen the app from the recents menu, verify that your ViewModel successfully restores the data saved within the SavedStateHandle."}]
